    "q-332029": {
      "dom_id": "step-q332029",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{-6(s-1)}{s(s-3)}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{2{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-3=2{X}_{1}(s)-{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+6=-2{X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$\n\nRewriting the system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(s-2){X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s)=3 \\\\ 2{X}_{1}(s)+(s-1){X}_{2}(s)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{-6-(s-1){X}_{2}(s)}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s-2)[\\frac{-6-(s-1){X}_{2}(s)}{2}]+{X}_{2}(s) & =3 \\\\ (s-2)[-6-(s-1){X}_{2}(s)]+2{X}_{2}(s) & =6 \\\\ -6(s-2)-(s-2)(s-1){X}_{2}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s) & =6 \\\\ [-(s-2)(s-1)+2]{X}_{2}(s) & =6+6(s-2) \\\\ (-{s}^{2}+3s){X}_{2}(s) & =6s-6 \\\\ -s(s-3){X}_{2}(s) & =6(s-1) \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{-6(s-1)}{s(s-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${X}_{1}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-6-(s-1){X}_{2}(s)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6-(s-1)(\\frac{-6(s-1)}{s(s-3)})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6+\\frac{6(s-1)^{2}}{s(s-3)}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6s(s-3)+6(s-1)^{2}}{2s(s-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(s+1)}{s(s-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{3(s+1)}{s(s-3)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{-6(s-1)}{s(s-3)}.\n$$",
